# Changelog

Changelog: 09-10-2026 18:32:48

Docs: links to the old stand-alone repositories (which no longer exist) now point at the apps' folders in the
suite repository (README and the scripts' header comments).

Changelog: 09-10-2026 18:20:11

Bisect and Unlock windows: about box: the project link pointed at the old stand-alone repository, which no longer exists (GitHub answers
404); it now opens the app's folder in the BC250 Bazzite Suite repository.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 17:51:51

Bisect and Unlock windows: only one instance of each runs per user. Starting one again brings its running
window to the front instead of opening a second one (bc250_core's instance module, copied into each package
until the apps move onto core).

Changelog: 09-10-2026 16:16:30

Cores Unlock window: the Status with sudo button and its tooltip are translated into German, Spanish, French,
Italian, Japanese, Polish, Russian and Chinese. The .qm files were rebuilt with pyside6-lrelease, which
reproduces the previous .qm files byte for byte, so only these two strings changed.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 13:56:58

bc250-cores-unlock.sh --status printed each systemctl answer twice when the service was not installed
("Service: not-found" and "not installed, inactive" and "inactive" on separate lines). It now prints one line,
e.g. "Service: not installed, inactive".

Cores Unlock window: new Status with sudo button. It runs the status as root, asking for the sudo password when
needed, so the core presence mask is always shown. The new strings are English in every language for now.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 13:17:34

README: the passwordless sudo setup now checks the drop-in with `sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/bc250-bisect`,
which must print "parsed OK", and says to edit it by hand only with `sudo visudo -f`. A malformed line in
a drop-in makes every sudo command print a warning, or stops sudo working at all. Docs only; the app
is unchanged.

## 0.2.0

The last two roadmap items: optional longer burn-in, and translated GUIs.
Also contains the pre-flight review before the first run on real hardware.

Added:

- Optional `mprime` (Prime95) torture test as a second load source: `--load
  stress-ng|mprime|both` (default `stress-ng`) and `--mprime-bin PATH` for binaries that
  aren't on `$PATH`. mprime runs `StressTester=1` with PrimeNet off, pinned to exactly the
  same CPUs as stress-ng, from a private working directory per item under
  `$STATE_DIR/mprime/<item>/`. With `--load both` the two tools run sequentially, never at
  the same time, so they don't fight over the same threads - an attempt then takes
  2 x `--time`. A torture error gives the attempt the new outcome `MPRIME-FAIL`.
- Optional `rasdaemon` integration: `--rasdaemon` reads `ras-mc-ctl --errors` before and
  after every attempt and records the delta; a new hardware error gives the attempt the new
  outcome `RAS-ERROR`. The capability is probed after the sudo preflight (the command needs
  root), and a missing or unusable `ras-mc-ctl` is reported once and then ignored. The full
  table is exported to the Desktop folder as `ras-mc-ctl-errors.txt`.
- `runs.tsv` gained two columns: the load tool of the attempt and its rasdaemon error count.
  Records are padded instead of rewritten, so result files from older versions (11 or 12
  columns) are still read correctly. The report, the per-item notes and the mixed-settings
  warning all know about the load tool now.
- Both new knobs are persisted in `config`, survive reboots, and are carried into the
  `--auto` user service's `ExecStart` line.
- Setup GUI: a "Load tool" combo box and an "Also count hardware errors with rasdaemon"
  checkbox, both reflected in the runtime estimate and the start confirmation.
- GUI translations for both front-ends: German, Spanish, French, Italian, Polish, Russian,
  Simplified Chinese and Japanese, with English as the source language. The language follows
  the desktop locale and can be forced with `--lang <code>`; Qt's own `qtbase_<lang>.qm` is
  loaded as well, so standard dialog buttons follow suit. Source `.ts` and compiled `.qm`
  catalogs live in each package's `translations/` directory and are installed with the
  package, so runtime needs nothing extra - only rebuilding them needs `pyside6-lrelease`
  (pip `PySide6-Essentials`). Terminal output of the shell scripts stays English.
- README: a "Longer burn-in (mprime / rasdaemon)" section and a "Languages" section; both
  remaining roadmap bullets struck through.
- `VERSION` and both scripts now read `0.2.0`.

Added (pre-flight review):

- New setup GUI `cores_bisect`: a one-screen launcher that turns the `-t`/`-r`/
  `--same-boot`/`--auto` options into a `bc250-cores-bisect.sh` command line, shows a runtime
  estimate, and then hands off to a real terminal so the script keeps its TTY and its prompts.
  Also exposes `--status`/`--reset`, the script's own `--help`, and can install the `--auto`
  auto-resume user service. Packaged with `packaging/bazzite/install-bisect-gui.sh`.

Fixed:

- The GUI's menu entry had no icon on Bazzite: `install-gui.sh` left the bare theme name in
  `Icon=`, which gamescope/freshly-logged-in sessions look up before the hicolor cache is
  rebuilt. The installers now write the icon's absolute path.
- `VERSION` and both scripts still said `0.1.0` while the repo was tagged `v0.1.9`, so the GUI
  reported a stale version. All three now read `0.1.9`.

- `--auto` was broken: `ask()` printed its info line to stdout, polluting the captured
  answer, so auto mode refused the unlock offer and bailed at the busy-system warning.
  Prompts now go to stderr; only the answer is captured.
- Reboots now fall back to `sudo -n systemctl reboot` (plain `systemctl reboot` fails
  polkit when run from the `--auto` user service), with a clear manual-reboot message.
- `cpu_freq` reported 0 MHz for range CPU specs (`0-15`, the control item): invalid sysfs
  path built from the range; now strips at `,` or `-`. Real-hardware testing also showed
  the BC-250 exposes no cpufreq scaling driver at all, so it now falls back to the
  `cpu MHz` field in `/proc/cpuinfo`.
- New-core detection fed `sort -n` output to `comm` (needs lexicographic order); safe only
  by luck with single-digit core_ids. Inputs now sorted lexically, result numerically.
- Missing pciutils was reported as "no BC-250 found"; `lspci`/`setpci` presence is now
  checked before the board check (both scripts).
- The sudo preflight used `sudo -v`, which prompts under the default `verifypw=all` when
  sudoers has mixed rules (e.g. wheel with password + a NOPASSWD drop-in) - in `--auto`
  this surfaced as a blocking ksshaskpass popup at every attempt. Now tries `sudo -n true`
  first and only falls back to `sudo -v`; the keepalive refreshes the same way.

Hardened:

- `core_mask` double-reads until two consecutive reads agree: the SMN index/data pair at
  0xB8/0xBC is shared with the governor, so a single racy read could silently return a
  wrong mask (both scripts).
- `--install` copies the script to `/usr/local/sbin` (root-owned) and points the boot
  service there, so root never executes a user-owned file; `--uninstall` removes the copy.
- Autostart example uses `WantedBy=default.target`: gamescope/Steam sessions don't
  reliably activate `graphical-session.target`.
- Release workflow restored (it was fully commented out, so every run failed as an invalid
  workflow) and adapted to this repo: correct archive name, no GUI/packaging references,
  and a `bash -n` + ShellCheck gate before releasing.

Added:

- GUI `cores_unlock` (PyQt6, English only for now), modeled on bc250-cu-bisect's GUI.
  It reads the bisect results from `~/.local/share/bc250-cores-bisect` and only enables
  Install when the verdict gate passes: bisect phase, no attempt in progress, control and
  post-control clean, every new core and the combined item PASS in every round. It shows a
  core map (stock / ok / fails / random / not tested), runs `bc250-cores-unlock.sh`
  `--install`/`--uninstall` through sudo (password dialog only when sudo needs one) and
  `--status` unprivileged. It never runs the bisect itself.
- `packaging/bazzite/install-gui.sh` / `uninstall-gui.sh`: per-user install (venv,
  launcher `bc250-cores-unlock-gui`, menu entry, icon) without rpm-ostree or root; the names
  don't clash with the bc250-cu-bisect GUI. `requirements.txt` for running it from a venv.
- Release workflow: also byte-compiles the GUI and ShellChecks the packaging scripts.

- Report and all raw data are copied to `~/Desktop/bc250-cores-bisect/` (like
  bc250-bazzite-test): the report, `runs.tsv`, `config`, the per-attempt stress-ng logs and
  `auto.log`. Previously only the report landed loose on the Desktop and the stress-ng logs
  stayed hidden in `~/.local/share/bc250-cores-bisect/logs`.

- Report accuracy (from the first overnight run, which mixed a 120 s quick test with 1800 s
  rounds while the header claimed 1800 s throughout):
  - Every attempt now records its load length (new last column in `runs.tsv`; files from
    older versions stay valid and show `unknown`). The summary shows a per-item
    "load per attempt" breakdown when an item's attempts didn't all run the current load.
  - Changing `-t`/`-r` partway through a run is announced before the new values are saved
    (and asks to confirm unless `--auto`), suggesting `--reset` for a uniform result.
  - After the unlock, the summary still lists the control rounds, and says so when the
    control ran fewer rounds than the current `--rounds`. A failed control now also blocks
    the "looks safe" verdict in the bisect phase.

- The core map is now drawn during the control phase too (previously bisect-only): before
  the unlock the fused-off cores are derived as the IDs missing from 0-7 and shown as `..`.
- `--auto` cleans up after itself: when all attempts are done it disables the autostart
  unit and removes the live-output autostart entry, so nothing starts at the next login.
  Symmetrically, a new `--auto` run re-enables the unit if it's installed but disabled, and
  warns when the unit isn't installed at all (the run couldn't continue past a reboot).
- App icon (`images/bc250-cores-bisect.png`, 512x512, transparent background).
- README: "How long does it take?" section with wall-clock estimates per invocation, and a
  "Running truly unattended" checklist for `--auto` (autologin, passwordless sudo setup,
  autostart unit, silencing KDE Wallet / ksshaskpass popups, how to monitor a run).

## 0.1.0

Initial scaffold.

- `bc250-cores-bisect.sh`: control (stock 6C/12T) -> SMU unlock -> per-core pinned bisect
  (post-control, each new core alone, combined) with stress-ng --verify, MCE detection,
  crash-safe multi-boot state machine, core map, verdicts, report and decision.
- `bc250-cores-unlock.sh`: reversible persistence - re-applies the volatile unlock after a
  cold boot via a root systemd service and warm-reboots once, with a reboot-loop guard.
- Own implementation of the SMN/SMU queue-3 access (setpci on 0000:00:00.0, index/data
  0xB8/0xBC); hardware facts credited to rw-r-r-0644/bc250-core-unlock.
- Untested on hardware yet: syntax-checked only. Treat as experimental.
